{"name":"Green Onyx","hex":"#989a82","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"onyx","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A banded variety of chalcedony, a cryptocrystalline form of quartz.","A jet-black color, named after the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Jet-black; often, glossily so."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/onyx"}],"entries":[{"title":"Onyx","blocks":[{"text":"Onyx is a typically black-and-white banded variety of agate, a silicate mineral. The bands can also be monochromatic with alternating light and dark bands. Sardonyx is a variety with red to brown bands alternated with black or white bands. The name \"onyx\" is also frequently used for level-banded (parallel-banded) agates, but in proper usage it refers to color pattern not band structure. Onyx, as a descriptive term, has also been incorrectly applied to parallel-banded varieties of alabaster, marble, calcite, obsidian, and opal, and misleadingly to materials with contorted banding, such as \"cave onyx\" and \"Mexican onyx\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Agate-Quartz-49959.jpg/330px-Agate-Quartz-49959.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onyx","wikidata":"Q180884"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Shades of black","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of black, or off-black colors, are colors that differ only slightly from pure black. These colors have a low lightness. From a photometric point of view, a color which differs slightly from black always has low relative luminance. Colors often considered \"shades of black\" include onyx, black olive, charcoal, and jet."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Color_icon_black_new2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_black","wikidata":"Q7460340"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg/500px-Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg/500px-Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cabochons of red onyx, also called sardonyx","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg/250px-Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg/250px-Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black onyx with white streaks","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Onyx.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/500px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/1280px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/500px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Gemma Augustea is a Roman cameo produced 9–12 AD and carved in a two-layered onyx gem (19 × 23 cm).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01